          "heading": "Foundational Principles & Curriculum Scope: Temperature Sensors: Thermocouples, RTDs, Thermistors, and Cold-Junction Compensation",
          "content": "# Unit 2 \u2014 Time, frequency, phase and synchronization\n\n**Conceptual thread:** Define timestamps before interpreting events.  \n**Application scenario:** A network of timestamped electrical pulses with a fictional timing reference.\n\n## Why this topic matters\n\nA frequency counter and a timestamping system may disagree without either having a defective oscillator. Finite gate time, threshold crossings, jitter, dead time and clock drift have separate mechanisms. Proper timing architecture is an engineering decision about what uncertainties matter, not simply about buying a faster counter. The engineering goal is a measurement whose physical interpretation can survive an independent check. The activity is intentionally model-based: any numbers in assignments, datasets or simulator plots are invented for educational purposes. In actual equipment, frequency-dependent calibration, laboratory safety and environmental ratings would require external evidence.\n\n## Learning outcomes\n\nBy the end of this unit, you should be able to construct the principal measurement relation and explain its limits; reproduce two independent numerical results; identify at least one coherent alternative explanation for an apparent trend; select the data and metadata needed for a reproducible report; and design a measurement assurance check that does not assume the result it intends to confirm. The last outcome is central: a collection of accurate-looking digital values is not itself a valid measurement.\n\n## Suggested sequence\n\nRead the first lesson as a model-building exercise, the second as an instrument or method comparison, the third as an error-analysis problem, and the fourth as a verification and decision exercise. Keep an equation notebook with a units check, an uncertainty table and a record of any assumptions you changed. Then solve the [unit assessment](../assessments/unit-02.html), complete the [integrated project](../projects/unit-02.html) and explore the [interactive model](../simulations/model-02.html).\n\n## Model interpretation\n\nThe simulator for this unit is **Clock drift accumulation**. Linear time-error accumulation from a constant oscillator frequency offset; stochastic jitter omitted. It varies only the mathematical quantities visible in its controls; a plotted result does not constitute an equipment qualification or measured sample. For a meaningful parameter study, save the default values, change one input at a time, reproduce a point independently and then look for a counterexample outside the ideal model. The derivative of the curve, not merely its appearance, is often the best clue about sensitivity and where resolution becomes inadequate.\n\n## Topics\n\n- [Lesson 2.1 \u2014 Frequency counters and finite gate time](../lessons/U02-L01-frequency-counters-and-finite-gate-time.html)\n- [Lesson 2.2 \u2014 Phase measurement and time skew](../lessons/U02-L02-phase-measurement-and-time-skew.html)\n- [Lesson 2.3 \u2014 Clock stability, drift and Allan deviation](../lessons/U02-L03-clock-stability-drift-and-allan-deviation.html)\n- [Lesson 2.4 \u2014 Trigger uncertainty, time intervals and PTP context](../lessons/U02-L04-trigger-uncertainty-time-intervals-and-ptp-context.html)\n\n> **Scope and safety.** Every dataset in this book is synthetic. Models are intended for offline learning, and do not authorize connection to energized power apparatus, unsafe test voltages, industrial machinery, RF transmitters, clinical devices, or safety-critical control systems. Consult qualified laboratory personnel, current equipment manuals, local rules and appropriate device ratings before any physical work. No certification or calibration is claimed.\n"
