          "solution": "**Interpretation.** This calculation is a model prediction under the stated conditions, not a newly observed experimental result. To audit it, write the substituted numbers next to their units, calculate without premature rounding, and estimate the order of magnitude before accepting the final digits. Distinguish a predicted change caused by a controlled parameter from an apparent change caused by sensor gain, instrument timing, filtering or normalization. If the expected sign of a correction is reversed, review the signal flow and physical reference before changing the equation to match the desired answer.\n\n### Checks that should accompany the numerical result\n\nFirst, change one input while keeping the remaining assumptions fixed and confirm the output moves in the physically expected direction. Second, calculate the result through a different arrangement of the same governing law or a limiting-case approximation. Third, state the span over which the model has been calibrated or independently verified. Fourth, document whether the result is meant to represent an instantaneous value, an RMS estimate, an average over a defined interval or a statistical bound. These distinctions affect what can legitimately be compared with a manufacturer's specification."
