          "heading": "Engineering Design Project & Numerical Investigation: Automated Test Systems, GPIB / IEEE 488, SCPI Protocols, and Virtual Instrumentation",
          "content": "# Project 8 \u2014 Industrial diagnostics and measurement assurance: reproducible measurement dossier\n\n**Scenario.** An offline condition-monitoring case using synthetic machine and thermal measurements. **Status:** synthetic design exercise with no physical implementation. Deliverable: a 5\u20138 page engineering dossier plus a numerical notebook or spreadsheet and reproducible model output. This project evaluates reasoning and communication, not ability to claim certification.\n\n## Part 1 \u2014 measurand and system architecture\n\nSpecify the intended measurand, realistic operating range and the decision it supports. Draw a signal chain identifying physical source, sensor or test interface, conditioning, clock, digitization and software. Explain which parts should be independently calibrated. Build two competing explanations for a suspicious result, one physical and one instrumentation-related. The two models must predict different responses to at least one controllable input.\n\n## Part 2 \u2014 baseline and sensitivity\n\nOpen the [interactive model for Unit 8](../simulations/model-08.html) and save its complete default inputs. Export the calculated sweep. Independently reproduce three points using the mathematical relation identified in the model. Vary at least two parameters in separate runs and compare the direction and magnitude of their effects. The response should be represented by a labeled graph and a table; neither may be presented as observed field measurements.\n\n## Part 3 \u2014 model discrepancy and uncertainty\n\nIntroduce one nonideal process grounded in the lessons: **Real contacts change resistance with temperature and may have nonlinear convection, differing airflow and thermal time constants. A steady-state model does not predict transient peak temperatures.**. State the size and justification of every invented parameter. Compute a first-order sensitivity where possible and distinguish a correction from a standard uncertainty. Identify a common-reference or shared-clock mechanism that could correlate results. Explain why repeated acquisitions on the same setup might fail to uncover this mechanism.\n\n## Part 4 \u2014 validation against an alternative mechanism\n\nConstruct a test that would tell the main model apart from the following limitation: **Coverage factor, measurement distribution, conformance risk and agreement with the customer all influence the chosen decision rule. A failed verification may indicate either measurement-system trouble or device change.**. Identify the reference observation, intervention, evidence to retain and what result would make the conclusion inconclusive. Specify an acceptance or escalation rule *before* inspecting results. Compare a plausible success case and a boundary case under that same rule.\n\n## Part 5 \u2014 audit-quality communication\n\nPresent a conclusion with three headings: supported by the synthetic model, unverified in a physical instrument, and next evidence required. Include raw data, equations, units, assumption register, uncertainty budget, exported CSV, software version and short reproducibility instructions. Do not include fabricated calibration certificates, genuine-looking measurement signatures or a suggestion that any component was tested in an accredited laboratory.\n\n## Marking rubric\n\n20% physics and sensing-chain architecture; 20% reproducible calculations; 20% error modeling and uncertainty; 20% discrimination between fault hypotheses; 20% accurate reporting and evidence hygiene. A beautiful graph with no independent calculation does not pass. An overly confident conclusion must be revised even when its central arithmetic is correct.\n\n> **Scope and safety.** Every dataset in this book is synthetic. Models are intended for offline learning, and do not authorize connection to energized power apparatus, unsafe test voltages, industrial machinery, RF transmitters, clinical devices, or safety-critical control systems. Consult qualified laboratory personnel, current equipment manuals, local rules and appropriate device ratings before any physical work. No certification or calibration is claimed.\n"
